      "side": 1,
      "message": "Coming back to the command fetch, as a new comment rather than reopening the resolved thread. The single fetch is right and the race is gone, thanks. The simplification does bring something else back though, and I think it is worth more than a style point: cmd holds the raw_cmd[] member, which we filled with le64_to_cpu, and the memcpy writes that into the queue the ITS reads little-endian, with no conversion on the way out. The driver\u0027s its_fixup_cmd does the cpu_to_le64 before a command reaches the queue for exactly this reason.\n\nOn little-endian both conversions are the identity, so nothing changes on anything we ship, and no test on the shipping config would ever catch it. On CONFIG_CPU_BIG_ENDIAN it is a validation bypass rather than a cosmetic bug: we validate the little-endian reading of the host\u0027s 32 bytes and write the byte-reversed form into the queue. The opcode we check is byte 0 of the first word, the opcode the ITS executes becomes byte 7, so the host picks a pair where the first reads as something we wave through (SYNC) and the second is a MAPD with an ITT address of its choosing. Every check in the series is bypassed at once.\n\narm64 does support big-endian even though Android does not build it, and the ITS half is heading upstream, so I would rather not leave it resting on le64_to_cpu happening to be an involution.\n\nI checked that process_cmd and everything under it only ever read cmd (the whole set is the six field extractions at :323-326, :354 and :363, and process_its_mapd\u0027s remove \u003d !remove is a local), so writing the decoded copy back is incidental rather than load-bearing. That means the raw snapshot form costs the same and makes \"the ITS executes the bytes we validated\" true by construction:\n\n```\n\tstruct its_cmd_block raw, cmd;\n\t...\n\t\tmemcpy(\u0026raw, its-\u003ecmd_host_base + its-\u003ecmd_offset, sizeof(raw));\n\n\t\tfor (i \u003d 0; i \u003c ARRAY_SIZE(cmd.raw_cmd); i++)\n\t\t\tcmd.raw_cmd[i] \u003d le64_to_cpu(raw.raw_cmd_le[i]);\n\n\t\tif (process_cmd(its, \u0026cmd, /* rollback */ false))\n\t\t\treturn;\n\n\t\tmemcpy(its-\u003ecmd_hyp_base + its-\u003ecmd_offset, \u0026raw, sizeof(raw));\n```\n\nOne thing that would flip this: if you ever want process_cmd to rewrite a field before submission, clamping the MAPD Size against GITS_TYPER.ID_bits being the obvious candidate, then the snapshot would silently drop the sanitisation and you want cpu_to_le64 on the way out instead. If that is where this is going, probably worth doing it that way now.",
      "revId": "d7808e3b5adf0c21af21f22effcae3db4f98cc00",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
